60,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, the candidates are out. Given the clay risk and your crossing data, which plan actually survives the ground and the people?","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, the clay risk rules out heavy earth-moving. Plan B’s shallow planting fits the two firms’ capabilities and the phasing constraints.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Mio,,"@Misaki, wait! Triple upkeep costs for planted ground? With only seven staff left, Plan B is a financial trap. I can’t support it.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, the volunteer groups handle habitat tasks, but they can’t mow turf or fix courts. Plan D’s hard surfaces match the firm ground people actually use.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, agreed. Plan D’s hard surfaces match the firm ground people actually use. The clay risk makes heavy earth-moving for C or B too dangerous.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, Plan D’s courts and terraces are hard surfaces, but the clay subsoil still threatens their foundations. I need to know if the earlier programme’s apron failures apply here.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, the apron failures were surface-level. Plan D’s shallow foundations avoid the deep clay issues, unlike the heavy earth-moving for C.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Benjamin, but hard surfaces are cheap to maintain! Plan D avoids the triple cost of planting. It’s the only one that won’t bankrupt us in three years.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, hard surfaces are cheaper, but the casual pool only covers weekends. Who maintains the courts daily? I need a staffing plan, not just a budget line.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, the crossing counts show people need firm, open ground. Plan D’s hard surfaces match that reality, unlike the soft margins they avoid.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mio, the apron failures were surface-level, but Plan D’s terraces require deeper excavation. If the clay slips, those foundations fail. I need to see if the shallow work is truly viable.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, the firms quote shallow work at ordinary rates. Plan D’s terraces are shallow, avoiding the deep clay risks that plagued the earlier apron.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Benjamin, if the terraces are shallow, the clay risk drops! But I still worry about the long-term upkeep. Can we really afford the courts?","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, the casual pool handles weekend stewarding, but daily court maintenance is bought-in. It’s a recurring cost, but far less than triple upkeep for planting.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Minako,,"@Mio, the recurring cost is manageable. The real risk is ignoring the crossing data. Plan D respects the firm ground people actually use, unlike the soft margins they avoid.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, I concede. The shallow work is viable, and the crossing data is undeniable. Plan D aligns with the ground and the people. I vote for D.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, agreed. The shallow foundations and phased hoarding make D the only viable option given the clay constraints. I vote for D.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Mio,,"@Benjamin, I’m still nervous about the long-term costs, but if the terraces are shallow and the upkeep is lower than planting, I can live with it. I vote for D.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, the staffing gap is real, but the volunteer groups and casual pool can cover the basics. Plan D is the only one that fits the operational reality. I vote for D.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Minako,,"@Benjamin, the data confirms it. Plan D respects the firm ground and the clay limits. I vote for D.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
